==================== CLAUDE ANALYSIS, OBJECTIVE CYCLE 2 (rounds 2-3) ====================
What happened: v3 council plus the attacker doctrine, 4 children x 20 generations per round (14.1 and 17.3 min). Both rounds
promoted c2 (+0.04 and +0.015 over the champion on round benchmarks), but on the fixed batch (90 matchups x 4 seeds x 6 opponents,
analysis_cycle2.txt) the result is flat: doctrine-only reference 71% win, trained champion 71%. Attack 73 -> 74%, defend 79 -> 79%,
plain 66 -> 66%. Small shifts: weaker attackers 56 -> 61%, weaker defenders 42 -> 48%, attack vs guard 85 -> 78%, defend vs orbit
74 -> 80%. These are within what one batch can distinguish, so I read the training as no gain: the promotions are noise again.

Defender follow-up: the loose leash did not help or hurt (79% either way), and the failures that remain are
specific matchups (for example 24 Fleas defending against a Wolverine and a Leveler lose every time vs charge and kite, even at
4x the power by DPS x HP). A fixed radius cannot be right when the enemy outranges the target, so I REMOVED the tether entirely;
defenders now have no objective rule and result in the same 79-80%. The objective-aware behaviour that works is the attacker's.

Conclusion for this stage: with the information the v3 council has, objective fights are done: attack 74%, defend 80% (plain 66%,
which is dominated by the weaker-side physics, not the objective). More training on the same inputs will not move it.
What could: (1) learnable objective inputs (tactical_v4: distance/health of both targets, enemies near our target) so a defender
can learn to intercept forward and an attacker can learn when to dive; (2) speed-advantage / turn-rate inputs for kite chasing;
(3) a shared platoon assignment ('these guard, these strike'). Recommendation: (1) first, because every objective failure I looked
at is a decision about WHERE to fight relative to the target, which the council cannot express.
The viewer already shows objective fights (the target appears as a structure with its own health bar); data/brains/obj/watch_config.json
builds a small viewer file: ./visigoth-simulator --catalog examples/t1-catalog.json --watch data/brains/obj/champion.json
--train-config data/brains/obj/watch_config.json --watch-out watch-objectives.html

==================== CLAUDE: UNITS RUNNING AWAY (user report) ====================
User observation: "a number of combat units run away from combat, dooming their fellows." scripts/flee_report.py replays battles and
classifies every decision (details in docs/FINDINGS.md). Short version: 40% of unit-time is movement away from the nearest enemy, 95%
of it from spacing's negative advance (never engagement) and 87% with the enemy already in weapon range, so mostly range-keeping by
ranged units; forbidding it did not help the win rate. The part that does abandon fellows is ABSENCE: 7.0% of unit-time over 1.5 ranges
from every enemy while a friend is in range of one, mostly slow vehicles and hovercraft and mostly when the platoon is not ahead.
Change: the out-of-contact pull now runs at full strength whenever a friend is already in weapon range of the target. Absence 7.0 -> 5.1%,
win rate 72 -> 73% on the fixed batch. If the user means kiting itself (artillery walking away), that is intentional range-keeping and
would be a design change; ask for the battle (scenario, opponent, time) to be sure.
